text:
Loop Workouts (Tuesday)
Four small programs. Predict the output before you compile any of them — that habit is the whole point, not the typing. Every one of these fits in a while loop and needs nothing you have not already met.

1 · Reverse the digits
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int n, rev = 0;

    printf("Enter n: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    while (n &gt; 0) {
        rev = rev * 10 + n % 10;   /* catch the last digit, and carry the rest left */
        n = n / 10;                /* throw that digit away */
    }

    printf("rev=%d\n", rev);
    return 0;
}
Two moves you already know: % 10 takes the last digit off, / 10 discards it. The only new idea is where a caught digit goes — rev * 10 shifts everything left to make room.
Try 1204 → 4021. Then try 900, and explain the answer before you object to it. And notice what the loop did to n: it consumed it. If you needed n afterwards, you would have to keep a copy — a loop that destroys its own input is a real bug in waiting.


2 · Sum of evens and odds, until −1
The first loop where the number of passes is not known in advance. It runs until the user says stop. That sentinel value, -1, is part of the specification — not a magic number you invented.
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int x, sum_even = 0, sum_odd = 0;

    printf("Enter numbers, -1 to stop: ");
    scanf("%d", &x);              /* read the FIRST one before the loop */

    while (x != -1) {
        if (x % 2 == 0)
            sum_even = sum_even + x;
        else
            sum_odd = sum_odd + x;

        scanf("%d", &x);          /* read the NEXT one at the end of the body */
    }

    printf("even=%d odd=%d\n", sum_even, sum_odd);
    return 0;
}
This is the read-ahead pattern, and the two scanf calls are not a mistake: the loop condition has to be able to look at a value before deciding whether to process it. Move the read to the top of the body and -1 gets added to a total — try it and see.
It is also the first time an if lives inside a loop. Say the invariant out loud: sum_even holds the total of every even number seen so far. If that sentence stays true every pass, the program is right.
Three questions worth arguing about:

What does -7 % 2 give? Is -7 counted as odd by this program? (Predict, then check.)
What happens if the very first number typed is -1?
What if the user types a letter? Where does scanf leave you, and how would you know?



3 · Count the passes
Take either program above and add one line — int passes = 0; — with passes++ inside the loop, and print it at the end. Now you can measure a loop instead of guessing about it. For the digit-reverser, how does the count relate to the number you typed?
That single habit — counting the passes — is what turns “this feels slow” into an engineering argument. There is a worked example of exactly that in the floor and ceiling notes, where counting is what exposes the difference between a loop that takes a billion passes and one that takes fifty.

Code You Can Copy
Every program below was compiled and run before it was put here; the outputs are what they actually printed. Predict each one before you run it — that is the exercise; the typing is not.

1 · One decision, then two — if / else
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int n;
    printf("Enter an integer: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    if (n % 2 == 0) printf("%d is even\n", n);
    else            printf("%d is odd\n", n);

    return 0;
}
7   -&gt;  7 is odd
-4  -&gt;  -4 is even
Note the test is n % 2 == 0, not n % 2 == 1. For -5, C gives -5 % 2 == -1 — so a program that asks “is the remainder 1?” quietly calls every negative odd number even. Lab 03’s drill 02 warned you.


2 · Many decisions in order — the else-if ladder
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int age, price;
    printf("Enter age: ");
    scanf("%d", &age);

    if      (age &lt; 5)  price = 0;
    else if (age &lt; 18) price = 60;
    else if (age &lt; 60) price = 150;
    else               price = 80;

    printf("price=%d\n", price);
    return 0;
}
3   -&gt;  price=0        12  -&gt;  price=60
30  -&gt;  price=150      70  -&gt;  price=80
The order of the rungs is the whole program. Each test is reached only when every test above it has already failed, which is why age &lt; 18 does not need to also say age &gt;= 5. Now swap the first two rungs and run it again: an 8-year-old pays 150.
Two questions to ask of any ladder: do my cases cover every possibility? and can two of them ever both be true?


3 · switch, where sharing a body is the point
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    char c;
    printf("Enter a lowercase letter: ");
    scanf(" %c", &c);          /* the space skips the leftover Enter */

    switch (c) {
        case 'a':
        case 'e':
        case 'i':
        case 'o':
        case 'u':
            printf("%c is a vowel\n", c);
            break;
        default:
            printf("%c is a consonant\n", c);
    }
    return 0;
}
e  -&gt;  e is a vowel        k  -&gt;  k is a consonant
A case with no statements simply falls into the next one. Five labels, one body. Here that is exactly what we want — remember this page when you meet the next one.


4 · The same mechanism, now a bug
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int input;
    printf("Enter an integer: ");
    scanf("%d", &input);

    switch (input % 2) {
        case 0:  printf("Number is even\n");
        case 1:  printf("Number is odd\n");
        default: printf("Neither even nor odd\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
10  -&gt;  Number is even
        Number is odd
        Neither even nor odd

5   -&gt;  Number is odd
        Neither even nor odd

-5  -&gt;  Neither even nor odd
Three lessons in one run. Without break, execution keeps falling through — 10 is even, odd, and neither. The entry point moves with the value, so 5 prints two lines. And -5 prints only the last line, because -5 % 2 is -1, which matches no case at all — that is why the default is there.
Add break; to the first two cases and run it again.


5 · switch on a character — the mini calculator
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    double a, b;
    char op;
    printf("Enter: a op b   (e.g. 6 * 7): ");
    scanf("%lf %c %lf", &a, &op, &b);

    switch (op) {
        case '+': printf("%g\n", a + b); break;
        case '-': printf("%g\n", a - b); break;
        case '*': printf("%g\n", a * b); break;
        case '/':
            if (b == 0) printf("cannot divide by zero\n");
            else        printf("%g\n", a / b);
            break;
        default:  printf("unknown operator '%c'\n", op);
    }
    return 0;
}
6 * 7   -&gt;  42
10 / 4  -&gt;  2.5
9 / 0   -&gt;  cannot divide by zero
5 ^ 2   -&gt;  unknown operator '^'
The / case carries an if inside it: dividing by zero is a precondition the operator cannot check for itself. And the default is what turns a typo into a message instead of silence.






Try it with int instead of double and ask for 10 / 4. You get 2. Week 3 is still collecting debts.





6 · break and continue in loops — is it prime?
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int n, i, divisor;

    while (1) {                          /* keep going until told to stop */
        printf("Enter a number 2..99 (anything outside stops): ");
        scanf("%d", &n);
        if (n &lt; 2 || n &gt; 99) break;      /* leaves the WHILE */

        divisor = 0;
        for (i = 2; i * i &lt;= n; i++) {
            if (n % i != 0) continue;    /* not a divisor - go to the next i */
            divisor = i;
            break;                       /* leaves the FOR, not the while */
        }

        if (divisor) printf("  %d is composite, smallest divisor %d\n", n, divisor);
        else         printf("  %d is prime\n", n);
    }
    printf("stopped\n");
    return 0;
}
97  -&gt;  97 is prime
91  -&gt;  91 is composite, smallest divisor 7
2   -&gt;  2 is prime
-1  -&gt;  stopped
Two breaks in one program, leaving two different loops. The first ends the whole session; the second ends only the divisor search. Neither reaches the other.
continue is the other word: it abandons this pass and goes to the next. Here it is only for illustration — if (n % i == 0) { ... } would do the same job — but it makes the shape visible.
Two things to try. Replace i * i &lt;= n with i &lt; n: still correct, and for 97 it takes 95 passes instead of 9. Then feed it 1, and decide whether the program’s answer is the one the specification wants.


7 · break to leave early — three attempts
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#define PIN 4242

int main(void) {
    int entered, attempt, ok = 0;

    for (attempt = 1; attempt &lt;= 3; attempt++) {
        printf("Enter PIN (attempt %d of 3): ", attempt);
        scanf("%d", &entered);
        if (entered == PIN) { ok = attempt; break; }
        printf("  wrong\n");
    }

    if (ok) printf("access granted on attempt %d\n", ok);
    else    printf("access denied after 3 attempts\n");
    return 0;
}
4242              -&gt;  access granted on attempt 1
1111 2222 4242    -&gt;  access granted on attempt 3
1 2 3             -&gt;  access denied after 3 attempts
This loop can end for two different reasons — the right PIN, or running out of attempts. After the loop, the program still has to know which. That is the whole job of ok: a loop that forgets why it stopped cannot report what happened.


8 · The trap — break inside a switch inside a loop
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    double balance = 1000.0;
    int choice, pass = 0;

    while (pass &lt; 3) {              /* bounded so we can watch the bug and still stop */
        pass++;
        printf("\n1 balance  2 deposit  3 withdraw  4 quit\nChoice: ");
        scanf("%d", &choice);

        switch (choice) {
            case 1: printf("balance = %.2f\n", balance); break;
            case 4: printf("quitting...\n"); break;      /* &lt;-- leaves the SWITCH */
            default: printf("not implemented\n");
        }
    }
    printf("loop ended after %d passes\n", pass);
    return 0;
}
Choose 4 — quit — three times:
quitting...
quitting...
quitting...
loop ended after 3 passes
It never quits. The break did exactly what it promised: it left the nearest enclosing construct, and that was the switch. The while never heard about it.
The fix is not a cleverer break. It is to give the loop something of its own to look at:
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    double balance = 1000.0, amount;
    int choice, running = 1;

    while (running) {
        printf("\n1 balance  2 deposit  3 withdraw  4 quit\nChoice: ");
        if (scanf("%d", &choice) != 1) break;      /* input ran out */

        switch (choice) {
            case 1:
                printf("balance = %.2f\n", balance);
                break;
            case 2:
                printf("amount: "); scanf("%lf", &amount);
                balance += amount;
                printf("balance = %.2f\n", balance);
                break;
            case 3:
                printf("amount: "); scanf("%lf", &amount);
                if (amount &gt; balance) printf("insufficient funds\n");
                else { balance -= amount; printf("balance = %.2f\n", balance); }
                break;
            case 4:
                running = 0;                       /* &lt;-- this is what ends the LOOP */
                printf("goodbye\n");
                break;
            default:
                printf("no such option\n");
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
1               -&gt;  balance = 1000.00
2, 500          -&gt;  balance = 1500.00
3, 2000         -&gt;  insufficient funds
4               -&gt;  goodbye
